#---
# purpose: "A map pattern's short binary key is read-only data, so deciding a head against it copies nothing onto the process heap"
#---
#
# A map pattern keyed by a binary -- `%{"key" => value}` -- is decided against
# those bytes. `describe/1` is called only with tuples, so its first clause
# answers every call and the map clause is never reached. The values come out
# of the mailbox, so each call is decided at run time against the whole head.
#
# The key is a short binary literal, which is read-only data the program ships,
# so no path copies it onto the process heap. `bitstring_allocs` counts the
# copies the three calls made: `0`.
def describe({a, b}), do: a + b
def describe(%{"key" => value}), do: value
def describe(_), do: 0

def relay() do
  receive do
    value -> value
  after
    0 -> 0
  end
end

def main() do
  send(self(), {1, 2})
  send(self(), {3, 4})
  send(self(), {5, 6})
  before = System.alloc_stats().bitstring_allocs
  describe(relay()) == 3 || raise("expected describe(relay()) == 3")
  describe(relay()) == 7 || raise("expected describe(relay()) == 7")
  describe(relay()) == 11 || raise("expected describe(relay()) == 11")
  count_after = System.alloc_stats().bitstring_allocs
  dbg(count_after - before)
end
